&nbsp;


Dear Peter,


&nbsp;


Thank you for your email.&nbsp; Please see the below answers to your questions.


&nbsp;


Open justice is a key pillar of English litigation, and important to the Claimants.&nbsp; We entirely understand your frustration and apologise that you have
 not been able to obtain copies of any documents that you have requested.


&nbsp;


You will see from the below answers that a usually straightforward principle of English litigation has been complicated in the &quot;Dieselgate&quot; litigation.&nbsp;
 Please note that the second answer is off&nbsp;the record/ for background only.&nbsp; We would be happy to speak further to explain the&nbsp;relevant judgment and order.


&nbsp;


Defendant contacts


&nbsp;


The Defendants are represented by a range of law firms, set out in the attached table.&nbsp; The Lead Defendants are Mercedes, Ford, PCD and Renault/Nissan,
 and some of the non-Lead Defendants share a distribution list.&nbsp; In order to email all Defendants, rather than writing to every one of the attached email addresses we email the following:


&nbsp;


mwfordukemissions@mcguirewoods.com;
team-pcd-cgshonly@cgsh.com; 
nissanhluk@hoganlovells.com; 
non-alglodefendants-external@freshfields.com; 
team-fca-cgshonly@cgsh.com; 
team-vauxhall-cgshonly@cgsh.com; 
hsfnoxlitigation@hsfkramer.com; 
nissandealers@taylorwessing.com; 
rad@signaturelitigation.com; 
sl-ukrenault@signaturelitigation.com.


&nbsp;


Answers to questions


&nbsp;


1. Can you confirm that as far as the claimants are concerned, the documents referred to in open court and in your skeleton argument, can (or should?)
 be made available to the public and press?


&nbsp;


As far as the Claimants are concerned, documents referred to in open court and the Claimants' skeleton argument are and should be available to any member
 of the public or press who request them.&nbsp; In his judgment dated 25 July 2025 (attached), Mr Justice Constable ruled that there was &quot;significant and justified public interest in this litigation&quot;, and that &quot;it should not be necessary for a member of
 the public or journalist to physically attend Court to understand the allegations&quot; (para 50).&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Whilst there is a distinction between types of documents used in the trial (&quot;Green&quot;, &quot;Amber&quot; or &quot;Red&quot;, denoting measures of confidentiality), Mr Justice
 Constable also ruled that &quot;there is&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;no practical difference in terms either of the workability of the trial or open justice between a designation of Green or Amber.&nbsp;[...]
Neither of these factors impinges upon the ability of the public and journalists to see, understand and report the contents of ‘Amber’ documents in precisely the same way as a ‘Green’ document during the course of a trial&quot; (para 76).


&nbsp;


There is a dispute between the parties as to both (i) what is a sufficient &quot;reference&quot; to a document to make it available to a requesting member of the
 public or press, and (ii) if a document has been referenced, which parts of that document have been referred to.&nbsp; The Claimants' position is that the fact of a journalist's request for a document is evidence of the public interest in that document, and that
 whole document should be provided.


&nbsp;


2. Can you explain in one or two sentences the legal principle that these documents should be made public? (I'd like to have a better grasp of the
 English legal system before I reach out to the court for a comment.)


&nbsp;


Please consider the answer to this question
off the record, as it is our explanation of the relevant principles to a tight deadline whilst in Court.


&nbsp;


It is an established principle of English law that, when a document is referred to in open Court, that document becomes public.


&nbsp;


There are two unique features of the &quot;Dieselgate&quot;/ Pan-NOx litigation to add to that principle:


&nbsp;


Collateral use



&nbsp;



The English Court rules state that &quot;a party to whom a document is disclosed may use the document only for the purpose of the proceedings in
 which it is disclosed, except&nbsp;[various exceptions&quot; (see rule 31.22 at 
PART 31 – DISCLOSURE AND INSPECTION OF DOCUMENTS – Civil Procedure Rules – Justice UK).&nbsp; In the English Court, parties who go to Court have legal duties to hand over relevant evidence, including evidence that is harmful to their own case or helps the other
 side.&nbsp; The rule against collateral use says that those documents can only be used in the Court case in which they were handed over, e.g., to stop people suing just in order to get evidence, and then using that evidence for ulterior motives.&nbsp; Once a document
 becomes public, the rule against collateral use falls away.&nbsp; Collateral use includes handing documents to journalists.



&nbsp;




The attached order of Mr Justice Constable dated 29 August 2025, at paragraph 12 deals with &quot;collateral use&quot; of Green and Amber documents.&nbsp; It
 says that there are no collateral use restrictions on anyone by the Claimants, i.e., us.&nbsp; What that means is: there is no ban on you seeing documents, but there is a ban on us handing documents to you
unless&nbsp;they have been referred to.


&nbsp;



The Defendants are arguing with us about what Green documents have actually been referred to; and what Amber documents have been referred to, and
 if so what parts of have been referred to.&nbsp; Unless we agree with the Defendants, or the Judge intervenes and tells us what has been referred to, there is a risk that we breach a Court order in handing to you a document that has not been referred to.


&nbsp;


Court folder


&nbsp;



The Court has asked us to establish a folder containing documents referred to in Court.&nbsp; We are in the process of setting that up.


    


&nbsp;


3. Can you give your explanation or assessment of why these documents have still not been made available, more than two weeks after the start of the
 trial?


&nbsp;


There are no restrictions on the use of documents referred to at trial by any parties,
except&nbsp;in respect of use the Claimants.&nbsp; There is a Court order that the Claimants may not pass documents to the public, journalists, etc. unless they have been referred to (the order of Mr Justice Constable dated 29 August 2025).


&nbsp;


It is not agreed between the parties which documents have been referred to nor, if they have been referred to, which parts of those documents.&nbsp; Absent
 agreement with the Defendants, or direction from the Court, the Claimants cannot pass documents to journalists without risking breaching a Court order.&nbsp; &nbsp;Therefore, whilst in theory (and in Mr Justice Constable's judgment and order) there is no ban on journalists
 seeing documents, in practice the restriction of the Claimants' use of documents is in practice passed on to journalists, as journalists can only get obtain them by being given them by the Claimants.


&nbsp;


