Dear all,


&nbsp;


More than two weeks after the trial has started I still have not received access to the documents mentioned in court. I discussed this with my editor and she agrees that this procedural situation in itself has become newsworthy.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


I will therefore write a post that will feature in our newsletter scheduled for Wednesday the 5th, if the documents are still not available by then. Hopefully asking questions on-the-record will put pressure on the involved parties
 to act (and if the documents are made public before Wednesday, the newsletter item will obviously be deleted).


&nbsp;


I therefore have two requests. One, if you could give me the names (and possibly contact details) of the lead lawyers of the defendants, so that I can ask them on-the-record if and why they object to the documents being made public.
 Two, if you could answer the following questions on-the-record:


&nbsp;


1. Can you confirm that as far as the claimants are concerned, the documents referred to in open court and in your skeleton argument, can (or should?) be made available to the public and press?
Yes


&nbsp;


2. Can you explain in one or two sentences the legal principle that these documents should be made public? (I'd like to have a better grasp of the English legal system before I reach out to the court for a comment.)
&nbsp;
The Courts recognise that open justice is “a fundamental principle of English law”. The Civil Procedure Rules govern the extent to which non-parties can access documents that are relied upon in cases before
 the Courts. For more information, see paragraphs 12 onwards of Mr Justice Constable’s judgment in this case:

Pan NOx De-Designation Judgment of the Managing Judge Constable


&nbsp;


3. Can you give your explanation or assesment of why these documents have still not been made available, more than two weeks after the start of the trial?
&nbsp;
As I am sure you appreciate the focus of the parties is currently on preparation for the ongoing hearing. Notwithstanding this, in the interests of open justice the parties have ongoing
 discussions regarding the provision of documents to third parties. There is a small number of documents (including those which you have requested) which the parties have agreed can be provided to third parties; the mechanism by which the documents are to be
 provided is still the subject of discussions/agreement, but we anticipate that this can be agreed in the coming days. Additionally, there are other categories of documents (again including other documents which you have requested) which the parties disagree
 can be provided to third parties, which dispute needs to be resolved.


&nbsp;


If you wish to comment, please reply by Friday 31 October noon&nbsp;the latest.


&nbsp;


All the best,


Peter


